  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Background Process**\n\nI\'ve just noticed an `ls -la` command went to the background. The system will alert us upon completion! The key takeaway here is that if the command\'s output isn\'t immediately displayed, it signifies that the command has been dispatched to the background.\n\n\n**Clarifying Termination Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the nuances of background task completion. I\'m focusing on the behavior of stopping tool calls when a non-daemon task is in progress. The documentation seems to suggest that the system automatically notifies me upon completion, even if I don\'t output any text. It\'s a key detail to confirm whether stopping tool calls ends the turn and returns to the user, or automatically wakes me up to process completion messages. I\'m going to look into this specific interaction, and will be sure to avoid premature returns!\n\n\n**Analyzing Turn Behavior**\n\nI\'m now reconsidering how turns behave with background tasks. The documentation states that background tasks send output as they run, and to stop calling tools to end the turn. I\'m focusing now on if this behavior returns to the user, or waits for task completion. I\'m also recalling `ls -la` and suspect the container restart after `ProvisionFirebase` was the bottleneck. I\'ll test these scenarios!\n\n\n"
