  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Deploying Fire Rules & Models**\n\nI\'ve successfully eliminated all ESLint errors. My current focus is deploying `firestore.rules` using `rpc_action` for the \"fax\" service and \"DeployRules\" method, done concurrently with the writing of TypeScript domain models in `src/types/lifeos.ts`, and core financial math functions in `src/utils/finance.ts`. The process has a decent flow, and is on schedule.\n\n\n**Expanding Default Model Parameters**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Master Prompt\'s details into the domain models. This includes setting up default user profiles, incorporating income sources, work schedules, sleep durations, and financial goals. I\'m also ensuring the correct currency, language, devices, and all default expense categories are built, along with the support for custom category options, by reviewing and expanding the parameters in `src/types/lifeos.ts`. The process is systematic. I am also working on `src/utils/energy.ts`, `src/utils/finance.test.ts`, `src/utils/energy.test.ts`, and `src/i18n/translations.ts` to include the specific unit tests and i18n support.\n\n\n**Defining Financial Invariants**\n\nI\'m solidifying the financial models. I\'m focusing on strict invariants: calculating available cash with only paid income minus expenses. I\'m also ensuring the correct calculation of pending receivables. Furthermore, I\'m integrating these rules into calculations for net worth, investment metrics, and budget status, while ensuring that the code strictly adheres to these invariant rules in `src/utils/finance.ts` and associated unit tests, and also including specific data type definitions in `src/types/lifeos.ts`.\n\n\n"
